  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how an additive, HDI, interacts with the surface of a lead halide perovskite lattice and is transformed into a more strongly binding species. The crystal framework is shown with blue Pb²⁺ ions surrounded by yellow I⁻ ions, while red spheres represent FA⁺ cations. Initially, HDI molecules sit at grain surfaces with reactive end groups exposed. The central message is that HDI reacts with FA⁺ at these grain boundaries or surfaces to form a new product, labeled FAHDI.\n\nThis conversion appears to improve interfacial binding: the unreacted HDI mainly shows limited coordination through its end groups, whereas the FAHDI product can anchor to the perovskite surface through multiple interactions. The inset indicates two types of bonding are important—coordination to Pb²⁺ and hydrogen bonding—suggesting that FAHDI bridges or passivates undercoordinated surface sites more effectively than HDI alone. Visually, the lower part of the figure emphasizes this denser, multi-point attachment of FAHDI across neighboring surface regions.\n\nOverall, the schematic conveys a surface-passivation mechanism in which chemical reaction with FA⁺ converts HDI into a species that better stabilizes perovskite grain surfaces.",
